{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QmpYAnMgQQtEovMSPmZTTh","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n     2→// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n     3→\n     4→// Tests for Stat, Lstat, and FileInfo field population (prd002-sys R2.1, R2.2, R2.3).\n     5→package sys\n     6→\n     7→import (\n     8→\t\"os\"\n     9→\t\"path/filepath\"\n    10→\t\"testing\"\n    11→\t\"time\"\n    12→)\n    13→\n    14→// TestStat_RegularFile verifies that Stat returns a fully populated FileInfo\n    15→// for a regular file with known properties (prd002-sys R2.1, R2.2).\n    16→func TestStat_RegularFile(t *testing.T) {\n    17→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n    18→\tpath := filepath.Join(dir, \"testfile\")\n    19→\tcontent := []byte(\"hello, stat\\n\")\n    20→\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, content, 0644); err != nil {\n    21→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating test file: %v\", err)\n    22→\t}\n    23→\n    24→\tfi, err := Stat(path)\n    25→\tif err != nil {\n    26→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n    27→\t}\n    28→\n    29→\t// Mode: must be a regular file with 0644 permissions.\n    30→\tif !fi.Mode.IsRegular() {\n    31→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.IsRegular() = false; expected true\")\n    32→\t}\n    33→\tif perm := fi.Mode.Perm(); perm != 0644 {\n    34→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.Perm() = %04o; expected 0644\", perm)\n    35→\t}\n    36→\n    37→\t// Size: must equal the written content length.\n    38→\tif fi.Size != int64(len(content)) {\n    39→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Size = %d; expected %d\", fi.Size, len(content))\n    40→\t}\n    41→\n    42→\t// Nlink: a freshly created regular file has exactly 1 hard link.\n    43→\tif fi.Nlink != 1 {\n    44→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Nlink = %d; expected 1\", fi.Nlink)\n    45→\t}\n    46→\n    47→\t// Uid/Gid: must match the current process (we created the file).\n    48→\tif fi.Uid != uint32(os.Getuid()) {\n    49→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Uid = %d; expected %d\", fi.Uid, os.Getuid())\n    50→\t}\n    51→\tif fi.Gid != uint32(os.Getgid()) {\n    52→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Gid = %d; expected %d\", fi.Gid, os.Getgid())\n    53→\t}\n    54→\n    55→\t// Dev: must be nonzero (every filesystem has a device number).\n    56→\tif fi.Dev == 0 {\n    57→\t\tt.Error(\"Dev = 0; expected nonzero device ID\")\n    58→\t}\n    59→\n    60→\t// Ino: must be nonzero.\n    61→\tif fi.Ino == 0 {\n    62→\t\tt.Error(\"Ino = 0; expected nonzero inode number\")\n    63→\t}\n    64→\n    65→\t// Blocks: must be nonnegative (a 12-byte file still occupies at least one block).\n    66→\tif fi.Blocks < 0 {\n    67→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Blocks = %d; expected nonnegative\", fi.Blocks)\n    68→\t}\n    69→\n    70→\t// Blksize: must be positive.\n    71→\tif fi.Blksize <= 0 {\n    72→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Blksize = %d; expected positive\", fi.Blksize)\n    73→\t}\n    74→\n    75→\t// ModTime: must be recent (within the last 60 seconds).\n    76→\tif time.Since(fi.ModTime) > 60*time.Second {\n    77→\t\tt.Errorf(\"ModTime = %v; expected within the last 60 seconds\", fi.ModTime)\n    78→\t}\n    79→\n    80→\t// Info: the underlying os.FileInfo must be populated.\n    81→\tif fi.Info == nil {\n    82→\t\tt.Error(\"Info is nil; expected non-nil os.FileInfo\")\n    83→\t}\n    84→\tif fi.Info.Name() != \"testfile\" {\n    85→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Info.Name() = %q; expected %q\", fi.Info.Name(), \"testfile\")\n    86→\t}\n    87→}\n    88→\n    89→// TestStat_HardLink verifies that Nlink increments when a hard link is created\n    90→// (prd002-sys R2.2).\n    91→func TestStat_HardLink(t *testing.T) {\n    92→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n    93→\tpath := filepath.Join(dir, \"original\")\n    94→\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(\"data\"), 0644); err != nil {\n    95→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating test file: %v\", err)\n    96→\t}\n    97→\tlink := filepath.Join(dir, \"hardlink\")\n    98→\tif err := os.Link(path, link); err != nil {\n    99→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating hard link: %v\", err)\n   100→\t}\n   101→\n   102→\tfi, err := Stat(path)\n   103→\tif err != nil {\n   104→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n   105→\t}\n   106→\tif fi.Nlink != 2 {\n   107→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Nlink = %d; expected 2 after creating one hard link\", fi.Nlink)\n   108→\t}\n   109→\n   110→\t// Both paths should share the same inode.\n   111→\tfiLink, err := Stat(link)\n   112→\tif err != nil {\n   113→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", link, err)\n   114→\t}\n   115→\tif fi.Ino != fiLink.Ino {\n   116→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Ino mismatch: original=%d, hardlink=%d; expected same\", fi.Ino, fiLink.Ino)\n   117→\t}\n   118→\tif fi.Dev != fiLink.Dev {\n   119→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Dev mismatch: original=%d, hardlink=%d; expected same\", fi.Dev, fiLink.Dev)\n   120→\t}\n   121→}\n   122→\n   123→// TestStat_NonexistentPath verifies that Stat returns a non-nil error for a\n   124→// path that does not exist (prd002-sys R2.1).\n   125→func TestStat_NonexistentPath(t *testing.T) {\n   126→\t_, err := Stat(\"/nonexistent/path/that/does/not/exist\")\n   127→\tif err == nil {\n   128→\t\tt.Error(\"Stat on nonexistent path returned nil error; expected non-nil\")\n   129→\t}\n   130→}\n   131→\n   132→// TestLstat_Symlink verifies that Lstat returns metadata for the symlink itself,\n   133→// not the target (prd002-sys R2.1, AC3).\n   134→func TestLstat_Symlink(t *testing.T) {\n   135→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   136→\ttarget := filepath.Join(dir, \"target\")\n   137→\tif err := os.WriteFile(target, []byte(\"target content\"), 0644); err != nil {\n   138→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating target file: %v\", err)\n   139→\t}\n   140→\tlink := filepath.Join(dir, \"symlink\")\n   141→\tif err := os.Symlink(target, link); err != nil {\n   142→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating symlink: %v\", err)\n   143→\t}\n   144→\n   145→\tfi, err := Lstat(link)\n   146→\tif err != nil {\n   147→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Lstat(%q): %v\", link, err)\n   148→\t}\n   149→\n   150→\t// The mode type bits must indicate a symlink.\n   151→\tif fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink == 0 {\n   152→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode = %v; expected ModeSymlink bit set for symlink\", fi.Mode)\n   153→\t}\n   154→}\n   155→\n   156→// TestLstat_RegularFile verifies that Lstat on a regular file (not a symlink)\n   157→// returns the same metadata as Stat (prd002-sys R2.1).\n   158→func TestLstat_RegularFile(t *testing.T) {\n   159→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   160→\tpath := filepath.Join(dir, \"regular\")\n   161→\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(\"data\"), 0644); err != nil {\n   162→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating test file: %v\", err)\n   163→\t}\n   164→\n   165→\tstatFi, err := Stat(path)\n   166→\tif err != nil {\n   167→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n   168→\t}\n   169→\tlstatFi, err := Lstat(path)\n   170→\tif err != nil {\n   171→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Lstat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n   172→\t}\n   173→\n   174→\t// For a regular file, Stat and Lstat should agree on all fields.\n   175→\tif statFi.Ino != lstatFi.Ino {\n   176→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Ino mismatch: Stat=%d, Lstat=%d\", statFi.Ino, lstatFi.Ino)\n   177→\t}\n   178→\tif statFi.Mode != lstatFi.Mode {\n   179→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode mismatch: Stat=%v, Lstat=%v\", statFi.Mode, lstatFi.Mode)\n   180→\t}\n   181→\tif statFi.Size != lstatFi.Size {\n   182→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Size mismatch: Stat=%d, Lstat=%d\", statFi.Size, lstatFi.Size)\n   183→\t}\n   184→}\n   185→\n   186→// TestStat_FollowsSymlink verifies that Stat follows a symlink and returns\n   187→// metadata for the target file, not the symlink itself (prd002-sys R2.1).\n   188→func TestStat_FollowsSymlink(t *testing.T) {\n   189→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   190→\ttarget := filepath.Join(dir, \"target\")\n   191→\tcontent := []byte(\"target data for stat\")\n   192→\tif err := os.WriteFile(target, content, 0644); err != nil {\n   193→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating target file: %v\", err)\n   194→\t}\n   195→\tlink := filepath.Join(dir, \"symlink\")\n   196→\tif err := os.Symlink(target, link); err != nil {\n   197→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating symlink: %v\", err)\n   198→\t}\n   199→\n   200→\tfi, err := Stat(link)\n   201→\tif err != nil {\n   202→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q) via symlink: %v\", link, err)\n   203→\t}\n   204→\n   205→\t// Stat follows the symlink; mode should be regular file, not symlink.\n   206→\tif fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n   207→\t\tt.Error(\"Stat on symlink returned ModeSymlink; expected regular file mode (should follow symlink)\")\n   208→\t}\n   209→\tif !fi.Mode.IsRegular() {\n   210→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.IsRegular() = false; expected true (Stat should follow symlink to target)\")\n   211→\t}\n   212→\tif fi.Size != int64(len(content)) {\n   213→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Size = %d; expected %d (target file size)\", fi.Size, len(content))\n   214→\t}\n   215→}\n   216→\n   217→// TestLstat_NonexistentPath verifies that Lstat returns a non-nil error for a\n   218→// path that does not exist (prd002-sys R2.1).\n   219→func TestLstat_NonexistentPath(t *testing.T) {\n   220→\t_, err := Lstat(\"/nonexistent/path/that/does/not/exist\")\n   221→\tif err == nil {\n   222→\t\tt.Error(\"Lstat on nonexistent path returned nil error; expected non-nil\")\n   223→\t}\n   224→}\n   225→\n   226→// TestStat_Directory verifies that Stat correctly identifies a directory\n   227→// (prd002-sys R2.2).\n   228→func TestStat_Directory(t *testing.T) {\n   229→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   230→\n   231→\tfi, err := Stat(dir)\n   232→\tif err != nil {\n   233→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", dir, err)\n   234→\t}\n   235→\tif !fi.Mode.IsDir() {\n   236→\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.IsDir() = false for directory; expected true\")\n   237→\t}\n   238→\tif fi.Ino == 0 {\n   239→\t\tt.Error(\"Ino = 0 for directory; expected nonzero\")\n   240→\t}\n   241→}\n   242→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"581c1e97-2a13-437e-bea5-05ca12759402","uuid":"9b89e541-a72a-48ad-a30d-97dcb710de5b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/192/pkg/sys/stat_test.go","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n// Tests for Stat, Lstat, and FileInfo field population (prd002-sys R2.1, R2.2, R2.3).\npackage sys\n\nimport (\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// TestStat_RegularFile verifies that Stat returns a fully populated FileInfo\n// for a regular file with known properties (prd002-sys R2.1, R2.2).\nfunc TestStat_RegularFile(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tpath := filepath.Join(dir, \"testfile\")\n\tcontent := []byte(\"hello, stat\\n\")\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, content, 0644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating test file: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tfi, err := Stat(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n\t}\n\n\t// Mode: must be a regular file with 0644 permissions.\n\tif !fi.Mode.IsRegular() {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.IsRegular() = false; expected true\")\n\t}\n\tif perm := fi.Mode.Perm(); perm != 0644 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.Perm() = %04o; expected 0644\", perm)\n\t}\n\n\t// Size: must equal the written content length.\n\tif fi.Size != int64(len(content)) {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Size = %d; expected %d\", fi.Size, len(content))\n\t}\n\n\t// Nlink: a freshly created regular file has exactly 1 hard link.\n\tif fi.Nlink != 1 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Nlink = %d; expected 1\", fi.Nlink)\n\t}\n\n\t// Uid/Gid: must match the current process (we created the file).\n\tif fi.Uid != uint32(os.Getuid()) {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Uid = %d; expected %d\", fi.Uid, os.Getuid())\n\t}\n\tif fi.Gid != uint32(os.Getgid()) {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Gid = %d; expected %d\", fi.Gid, os.Getgid())\n\t}\n\n\t// Dev: must be nonzero (every filesystem has a device number).\n\tif fi.Dev == 0 {\n\t\tt.Error(\"Dev = 0; expected nonzero device ID\")\n\t}\n\n\t// Ino: must be nonzero.\n\tif fi.Ino == 0 {\n\t\tt.Error(\"Ino = 0; expected nonzero inode number\")\n\t}\n\n\t// Blocks: must be nonnegative (a 12-byte file still occupies at least one block).\n\tif fi.Blocks < 0 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Blocks = %d; expected nonnegative\", fi.Blocks)\n\t}\n\n\t// Blksize: must be positive.\n\tif fi.Blksize <= 0 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Blksize = %d; expected positive\", fi.Blksize)\n\t}\n\n\t// ModTime: must be recent (within the last 60 seconds).\n\tif time.Since(fi.ModTime) > 60*time.Second {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"ModTime = %v; expected within the last 60 seconds\", fi.ModTime)\n\t}\n\n\t// Info: the underlying os.FileInfo must be populated.\n\tif fi.Info == nil {\n\t\tt.Error(\"Info is nil; expected non-nil os.FileInfo\")\n\t}\n\tif fi.Info.Name() != \"testfile\" {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Info.Name() = %q; expected %q\", fi.Info.Name(), \"testfile\")\n\t}\n}\n\n// TestStat_HardLink verifies that Nlink increments when a hard link is created\n// (prd002-sys R2.2).\nfunc TestStat_HardLink(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tpath := filepath.Join(dir, \"original\")\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(\"data\"), 0644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating test file: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tlink := filepath.Join(dir, \"hardlink\")\n\tif err := os.Link(path, link); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating hard link: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tfi, err := Stat(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n\t}\n\tif fi.Nlink != 2 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Nlink = %d; expected 2 after creating one hard link\", fi.Nlink)\n\t}\n\n\t// Both paths should share the same inode.\n\tfiLink, err := Stat(link)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", link, err)\n\t}\n\tif fi.Ino != fiLink.Ino {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Ino mismatch: original=%d, hardlink=%d; expected same\", fi.Ino, fiLink.Ino)\n\t}\n\tif fi.Dev != fiLink.Dev {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Dev mismatch: original=%d, hardlink=%d; expected same\", fi.Dev, fiLink.Dev)\n\t}\n}\n\n// TestStat_NonexistentPath verifies that Stat returns a non-nil error for a\n// path that does not exist (prd002-sys R2.1).\nfunc TestStat_NonexistentPath(t *testing.T) {\n\t_, err := Stat(\"/nonexistent/path/that/does/not/exist\")\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\tt.Error(\"Stat on nonexistent path returned nil error; expected non-nil\")\n\t}\n}\n\n// TestLstat_Symlink verifies that Lstat returns metadata for the symlink itself,\n// not the target (prd002-sys R2.1, AC3).\nfunc TestLstat_Symlink(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\ttarget := filepath.Join(dir, \"target\")\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(target, []byte(\"target content\"), 0644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating target file: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tlink := filepath.Join(dir, \"symlink\")\n\tif err := os.Symlink(target, link); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating symlink: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tfi, err := Lstat(link)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Lstat(%q): %v\", link, err)\n\t}\n\n\t// The mode type bits must indicate a symlink.\n\tif fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink == 0 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode = %v; expected ModeSymlink bit set for symlink\", fi.Mode)\n\t}\n}\n\n// TestLstat_RegularFile verifies that Lstat on a regular file (not a symlink)\n// returns the same metadata as Stat (prd002-sys R2.1).\nfunc TestLstat_RegularFile(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tpath := filepath.Join(dir, \"regular\")\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(\"data\"), 0644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating test file: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tstatFi, err := Stat(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n\t}\n\tlstatFi, err := Lstat(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Lstat(%q): %v\", path, err)\n\t}\n\n\t// For a regular file, Stat and Lstat should agree on all fields.\n\tif statFi.Ino != lstatFi.Ino {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Ino mismatch: Stat=%d, Lstat=%d\", statFi.Ino, lstatFi.Ino)\n\t}\n\tif statFi.Mode != lstatFi.Mode {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode mismatch: Stat=%v, Lstat=%v\", statFi.Mode, lstatFi.Mode)\n\t}\n\tif statFi.Size != lstatFi.Size {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Size mismatch: Stat=%d, Lstat=%d\", statFi.Size, lstatFi.Size)\n\t}\n}\n\n// TestStat_FollowsSymlink verifies that Stat follows a symlink and returns\n// metadata for the target file, not the symlink itself (prd002-sys R2.1).\nfunc TestStat_FollowsSymlink(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\ttarget := filepath.Join(dir, \"target\")\n\tcontent := []byte(\"target data for stat\")\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(target, content, 0644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating target file: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tlink := filepath.Join(dir, \"symlink\")\n\tif err := os.Symlink(target, link); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating symlink: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tfi, err := Stat(link)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q) via symlink: %v\", link, err)\n\t}\n\n\t// Stat follows the symlink; mode should be regular file, not symlink.\n\tif fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n\t\tt.Error(\"Stat on symlink returned ModeSymlink; expected regular file mode (should follow symlink)\")\n\t}\n\tif !fi.Mode.IsRegular() {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.IsRegular() = false; expected true (Stat should follow symlink to target)\")\n\t}\n\tif fi.Size != int64(len(content)) {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Size = %d; expected %d (target file size)\", fi.Size, len(content))\n\t}\n}\n\n// TestLstat_NonexistentPath verifies that Lstat returns a non-nil error for a\n// path that does not exist (prd002-sys R2.1).\nfunc TestLstat_NonexistentPath(t *testing.T) {\n\t_, err := Lstat(\"/nonexistent/path/that/does/not/exist\")\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\tt.Error(\"Lstat on nonexistent path returned nil error; expected non-nil\")\n\t}\n}\n\n// TestStat_Directory verifies that Stat correctly identifies a directory\n// (prd002-sys R2.2).\nfunc TestStat_Directory(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\n\tfi, err := Stat(dir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"Stat(%q): %v\", dir, err)\n\t}\n\tif !fi.Mode.IsDir() {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"Mode.IsDir() = false for directory; expected true\")\n\t}\n\tif fi.Ino == 0 {\n\t\tt.Error(\"Ino = 0 for directory; expected nonzero\")\n\t}\n}\n","numLines":242,"startLine":1,"totalLines":242}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FtCwRjJBAL1PVemf6xjyzd","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd003-format\n     2→title: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n     6→  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n     7→  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n     8→  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n     9→  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n    10→  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n    11→  harder.\n    12→\n    13→  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n    14→  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n    15→  not speculation.\n    16→\n    17→goals:\n    18→  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n    19→  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    20→  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n    21→\n    22→requirements:\n    23→  R1:\n    24→    title: Column alignment\n    25→    items:\n    26→      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n    27→      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n    28→      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n    29→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n    30→\n    31→  R2:\n    32→    title: ANSI color\n    33→    items:\n    34→      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n    35→      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n    36→      - R2.3: |\n    37→          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n    38→      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n    39→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n    40→      - R2.6: |\n    41→          Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n    42→          detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n    43→          sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n    44→          empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n    45→          utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n    46→      - R2.7: |\n    47→          Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n    48→          SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n    49→          must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n    50→          the override across test cases.\n    51→\n    52→  R3:\n    53→    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    54→    items:\n    55→      - R3.1: |\n    56→          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n    57→\n    58→            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n    59→                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n    60→            }\n    61→      - R3.2: |\n    62→          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n    63→      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n    64→      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n    65→      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n    66→      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n    67→\n    68→non_goals:\n    69→  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n    70→  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n    71→  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n    72→  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n    73→\n    74→acceptance_criteria:\n    75→  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n    76→  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n    77→  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n    78→  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n    79→  - \"AC5: SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n    80→  - \"AC6: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    81→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"581c1e97-2a13-437e-bea5-05ca12759402","uuid":"175e9f48-c495-4656-85ff-cc8a15f9872a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/192/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml","content":"id: prd003-format\ntitle: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n  harder.\n\n  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n  not speculation.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Column alignment\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: ANSI color\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n      - R2.3: |\n          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n      - R2.6: |\n          Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n          detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n          sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n          empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n          utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n      - R2.7: |\n          Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n          SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n          must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n          the override across test cases.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n\n            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n            }\n      - R3.2: |\n          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n  - \"AC5: SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":81,"startLine":1,"totalLines":81}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MamYtKJTWM3qXjed5P8BBn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of:\n1. The PRD requirements (R1 for columns/padding, R3 for human-readable sizes)\n2. The existing test style (from stat_test.go)\n3. The existing implementations (columns.go and size.go provided inline)\n\nLet me now create the two test files. I'll use table-driven tests with t.Run subtests as specified.\n\nFor `columns_test.go`:\n- Test Columns with empty input (returns nil)\n- Test Columns with single entry\n- Test Columns with entries fitting in one row\n- Test Columns with entries forcing single-column layout (wider than terminal)\n\n\n- Verify column-major ordering\n- Test PadRight and PadLeft with ASCII, multi-byte UTF-8, and strings at or exceeding the target width\n\nFor `size_test.go`:\n- Test HumanSize in both binary (1024-based) and SI (1000-based) modes\n- Verify it returns \"0\" for zero bytes and plain integers for values below the first threshold\n- Check decimal formatting rules where values under 10 get one decimal place and those 10 and above get none\n\nNow I'm working through how the Columns function actually arranges entries. It uses a column-major layout with a 2-space gap between columns, trying progressively fewer columns until it finds the maximum that fits within the terminal width. For a given number of columns and rows, each entry's position is determined by dividing its index by the number of rows to get the column, then using the remainder for the row. Let me trace through a concrete example with five entries and a 20-character width to verify the math. Let me recalculate with 3 columns instead—I need to figure out which entries go in each column by dividing the index by the number of rows, so with 2 rows, entries 0-1 are in column 0, entries 2-3 in column 1, and entry 4 in column 2, giving me widths of 2, 3, and 1 respectively.\n\nNow trying 2 columns with 3 rows gives me a total width of 7 with padding, which fits within the limit, so that becomes my best option. The layout would then arrange entries row by row, placing \"a\" and \"d\" in the first row.\n\nLet me also work through the HumanSize formatting logic for binary mode, checking how values scale across different thresholds like 1024 and 10240. I'm verifying the calculation for 1000000 in SI mode—it divides down to 1.0 at level 2, giving \"1.0MB\" which matches the requirement. Now I'll write out the test cases.\n\nWriting test code...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3747,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2527,"cache_read_input_tokens":38781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2527},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"581c1e97-2a13-437e-bea5-05ca12759402","uuid":"adc56bb4-391d-4b6d-b460-bdac68702c84"}
